# G045 — Site Checker with Time Scheduling

*v2. See `site-checker.md` for prose: the convergence project (alarm
+ news-ticker + weather + port-scanner + mail-checker), four-state
health (up/down/degraded/unknown), consecutive failures as confidence
metric, alerts fire on state transitions not states.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Infrastructure Health Dashboard

[infra-health
    IN ORDER TO
        check a dozen endpoints every 5 minutes concurrently
        classify each into up/down/degraded/unknown
        and alert only when status actually transitions

    every 5 minutes
        concurrent [
            em-site: @em <- [check {url: "https://eckenrodemuziekopname.com",
                                     expect: 200}]
        |
            wiki:    @wk <- [check {url: "https://wiki.eckenrodemuziekopname.com",
                                     expect: 200}]
        |
            api:     @api <- [check {url: "http://144.126.251.126:8080/health",
                                      expect: 200}]
        |
            db:      @db  <- [check-tcp {host: "144.126.251.126", port: 5432}]
        ]
        join

        @dashboard <- [compose {sites: [@em, @wk, @api, @db]}]

        for @s in @dashboard:sites
            @prev <- [history[last-status {site: @s:url}]]
            @s:status
                where not equal to @prev
                    -> [alert[dispatch
                                {to: "nathan",
                                 channel: "urgent",
                                 message: @s:url + ": " +
                                           @prev + " -> " + @s:status}]]

        [history[append
                  {sites: @dashboard:sites,
                   ts: [clock[now]]}]]
]

---

## Verb Surface

[check
    http            {url, expect, timeout-ms}    ;; -> Status
    tcp             {host, port, timeout-ms}      ;; -> Status
    compose         {sites}                        ;; Dashboard
]

[history
    append          {sites, ts}
    last-status     {site}
    consecutive-failures {site}
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: four-state health `where` speed matters too

[check[http {url: @u, expect: @exp, timeout-ms: @t}]
    IN ORDER TO
        decide up/down/degraded/unknown by combining correctness and timing
        because a slow response is different from no response

    attempt HTTP GET @u with timeout @t
        where status is @exp
          and elapsed less than @t / 2              -> up
        where status is @exp
          and elapsed between @t / 2 and @t         -> degraded
        where status is not @exp                    -> down
        where timeout fires                          -> down
        where no attempt yet                         -> unknown
]

G038's port scanner had three states; G045 adds *degraded* — the
site responds but not well enough. Choreographies need to score
both success AND quality.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: alert on transition `where` status changed

[alert[should-fire
        {site: @s, prev: @prev, current: @cur, consecutive: @n}]
    where @cur is not equal to @prev
        -> fire (state transition is the signal)
    where @cur is "down" and @n is at least 3
        -> fire (confidence threshold reached)
    otherwise
        -> no-op (steady state; one-off failures are noise)
]

One failure is noise; three is a pattern. The consecutive counter is
a confidence metric — the same discipline NTP uses over multiple
samples. Alerts fire on the EDGE, not the state.

---

## Demo

[demo
    @s <- [check[http
                   {url: "http://127.0.0.1:8080/health",
                    expect: 200, timeout-ms: 2000}]]
    -> @s:status        ;; -> "up" | "degraded" | "down"

    [history[append {sites: [@s], ts: 1000}]]
    [history[append {sites: [{url: @s:url, status: "down"}],
                      ts: 2000}]]
    -> [alert[should-fire
                {site: @s:url, prev: "up", current: "down",
                 consecutive: 1}]]
    ;; -> true (transition up→down, fire regardless of consecutive)
]
